A practical weekly plan

Since you mentioned your coding time is limited but you'd enjoy meaningful, lower-energy work, I'd shift the balance away from coding:

1 hour: Improve one feature on the website (only if it supports a business goal).
2 hours: Research one niche (market size, affiliate programs, regulations, competitors).
2 hours: Reach out to 3–5 organizations or businesses for conversations.
1 hour: Write one high-quality article or destination guide to improve search traffic.
30 minutes: Record what you learned and decide the next experiment.

Notice that only about one-sixth of the time is spent coding.

The strategy I'd follow

If I were advising this as a startup rather than a hobby, I'd pursue this sequence:

Build traffic with genuinely useful beach and marine content.
Add affiliate products that naturally fit each destination or activity.
Build relationships with reputable tourism operators, conservation organizations, and marine professionals.
Identify one operational problem that organizations repeatedly mention.
Build a focused software product to solve that problem, rather than adding more general website features.

That approach gives you multiple chances to create value. Even if the SaaS idea takes years to mature, the content and affiliate business can continue growing, and the relationships you build will make future products far more likely to solve real problems.

Looking at what you've already built, I don't think the investment has been wasted. I think you're at the point where the next dollar of value is much more likely to come from customer discovery than from another feature. The beach database and species guide are already enough to start conversations with potential partners and validate which direction has real commercial demand.


